{"page_content": "5 \n \nRationale for microgravity remains the same for all the droplet combustion experiments conducted onboard \nthe ISS. In microgravity the droplets burn in a spherically symmetric form, and therefore it is possible to \nmodel the processes with complex chemical kinetic mechanisms involving thousands of reactions leading to \na better understanding of the chemistry.  Moreover, the cool flames need much longer residence times and \ncannot be observed under normal gravity conditions with droplet flames.  \nc. NRA the Investigation was Selected Under \nThis project was initiated by NASA through a Science Definition Team (SDT) which formulated the overall \nobjectives. It was peer reviewed by an independent panel of scientists and approved by NASA HQ as a \nfollow-up experiments to FLEX, and FLEX-2 in the CIR using additional fuels and gases needed to complete \nthe test matrix. The experimental hardware is same as that of the FLEX experiments with few minor \nmodifications in the data acquisition systems.  \nd. Final Experiment Objectives \nExamine the cool flame combustion behavior of n-dodecane, n-dodecane/iso-dodecane fuel mixtures, and \nfarnesane fuels over a rage of pressure from 0.25 atm to 5 atm.  \n4.  Operations  \na. Procedures \nThe operational procedure used in the CFI tests is the same as for the FLEX experiments. Once the ISS crew \ninstalls the required fuel syringe, and the gas bottles containing the gas mixtures in the MDCA and CIR \nfacilities, all the actual experimental operations are conducted from the ground at the Telescience Support \nCenter (TSC) located at the NASA Glenn Research Center.  Prior to each test the combustion chamber is filled \nwith the specified gas mixture at the required pressure, and the hot-wire igniters are positioned close to the \ndroplet deployment site.  Subsequently, a droplet of desired size is dispensed between the hypodermic \nneedles and freely deployed, and ignited.  The igniter is withdrawn 